# Alternative code designs (not realised)

There are two high-level concepts how we can realise a mixed formulation:

1. We can realise the solver for the primary variable and the secondary 
   (@f$ p @f$) one as completely independent solvers, which are coupled only 
   within the cells. That is, we solve for @f$ u @f$ treating @f$ p @f$ as a 
   "material" parameter, and we solve, at the same time for @f$ p @f$ treating
   @f$ u @f$ as material parameter. As we do it concurrently, we get a
   system splitting similar to a Jacobi smoother.   
   
   In such a mindset, we would not couple the two Riemann problems on
   the faces either.
2. We realise the solver as one solver for a system of partial differential
   equations. This is a monolithic approach. 

 
 


# Implement the functionality
 
Upon the first run of the Python script, we get two new C++ classes, i.e. four
files: AbstractMyDG.h, AbstractMyDG.cpp, MyDG.h, MyDG.cpp. The interesting file
for the user is MyDG.cpp


